{"kind":"FETCH","data":{"headers":{},"body":"{\"id\":13712895,\"site\":\"lawnsguide\",\"type\":\"lawn-care\",\"short_title\":\"lawn-care-tips-in-hot-summer\",\"language\":\"en\",\"published_time\":\"2026-08-28 00:24:32\",\"modified_time\":\"2026-08-28 00:24:32\",\"author\":\"Anna Kowalski\",\"img\":\"https://s.alicdn.com/@sc02/kf/A754638074cdd4502a8ec247b764693ad1.jpg\",\"title\":\"Lawn Care Tips in Hot Summer: Keep Your Grass Green & Healthy\",\"description\":\"Discover essential lawn care tips for hot summer months. Learn proper watering, mowing, and maintenance techniques to keep your lawn thriving in extreme heat.\",\"url\":\"/lawn-care/lawn-care-tips-in-hot-summer\",\"body\":\"<h1>Lawn Care Tips in Hot Summer: Keep Your Grass Green &amp; Healthy</h1>\\n\\n<p>When temperatures soar and the sun beats down relentlessly, your lawn faces some of its toughest challenges of the entire year. Hot summer weather can quickly transform a lush, green yard into a patchy, brown eyesore if you don't adjust your lawn care routine accordingly. The good news? With the right strategies and a little extra attention, you can keep your grass healthy, resilient, and attractive even during the most scorching months.</p>\\n\\n<p>Summer lawn care isn't about doing <em>more</em> — it's about doing things <em>smarter</em>. From adjusting your mowing height to fine-tuning your watering schedule, small changes can make an enormous difference in how your lawn handles heat stress. Whether you're working with <a href=\\\"/grass-types/bermuda-grass\\\">warm-season Bermuda grass</a>, <a href=\\\"/grass-types/zoysia-grass\\\">Zoysia</a>, or <a href=\\\"/grass-types/tall-fescue\\\">cool-season fescue</a>, the principles of summer lawn care remain consistent: protect the roots, conserve moisture, and minimize stress.</p>\\n\\n<p>In this comprehensive guide, we'll walk you through every essential step for maintaining a beautiful lawn during hot summer weather. You'll learn actionable techniques backed by turf science, discover the best tools and products for the job, and understand the seasonal timing that separates thriving lawns from struggling ones.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Watering Your Lawn Correctly During Extreme Heat</h2>\\n\\n<p>Proper watering is the single most important factor in keeping your lawn alive and healthy during hot summer months. However, many homeowners make critical mistakes that either waste water or inadvertently harm their grass. Understanding the science behind summer irrigation will help you develop a watering routine that promotes deep root growth and drought resilience.</p>\\n\\n<h3>How Much Water Does Your Lawn Need in Summer?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Most lawns require between 1 and 1.5 inches of water per week during peak summer heat. This includes both rainfall and irrigation. The goal is to moisten the soil to a depth of 6 to 8 inches, which encourages roots to grow deeper where moisture is more stable and temperatures are cooler.</p>\\n\\n<p>To measure how much water your sprinkler system delivers, place several empty tuna cans or rain gauges across your lawn and run your system for 15 minutes. Measure the water collected, then calculate how long you need to run your system to deliver the recommended amount. This simple test eliminates guesswork and helps you avoid both underwatering and overwatering.</p>\\n\\n<h3>The Deep and Infrequent Watering Rule</h3>\\n\\n<p>One of the most common mistakes homeowners make during summer is watering lightly every day. While this might seem logical, shallow daily watering encourages roots to stay near the soil surface where they're vulnerable to heat and drought. Instead, water deeply two to three times per week. This trains your grass to develop deep root systems that can access moisture stored deeper in the soil profile.</p>\\n\\n<p>For <a href=\\\"/grass-types/kentucky-bluegrass\\\">Kentucky bluegrass</a> and other cool-season grasses, deep watering is especially critical because these varieties are more prone to summer dormancy. <a href=\\\"/grass-types/st-augustine-grass\\\">St. Augustine grass</a> and <a href=\\\"/grass-types/centipede-grass\\\">centipede grass</a> also benefit enormously from deep, infrequent irrigation during heat waves.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Best Time of Day to Water Your Lawn in Summer</h3>\\n\\n<p>Always water your lawn in the early morning, ideally between 4:00 AM and 8:00 AM. Morning watering offers several critical advantages:</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Reduced evaporation:</strong> Cooler morning temperatures and lower wind speeds mean more water reaches the root zone instead of evaporating into the air.</li>\\n<li><strong>Disease prevention:</strong> Grass blades dry quickly once the sun rises, reducing the window of time that moisture sits on leaves — a primary cause of fungal diseases like brown patch and dollar spot.</li>\\n<li><strong>Better pressure:</strong> Municipal water systems typically have higher pressure in the early morning before peak usage begins.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p>Avoid watering in the evening or at night. While evaporation is low, prolonged leaf wetness creates ideal conditions for fungal pathogens. Similarly, midday watering wastes significant amounts of water to evaporation and can even scorch grass blades in extreme conditions.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Signs Your Lawn Needs Water</h3>\\n\\n<p>Learn to read your lawn's signals. Footprints or mower tracks that remain visible for several minutes indicate that grass blades have lost turgor pressure and need water immediately. A bluish-gray cast across the lawn is another reliable indicator of moisture stress. If you notice these signs, don't wait for your scheduled watering day — irrigate right away.</p>\\n\\n<p>Invest in a soil moisture probe or a smart irrigation controller to take the guesswork out of watering. These tools measure actual soil conditions and adjust watering schedules automatically, ensuring your lawn gets exactly what it needs without waste. For more details on building a year-round watering plan, check out our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/lawn-watering-guide\\\">complete lawn watering guide</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Mowing Strategies to Protect Your Lawn From Heat Stress</h2>\\n\\n<p>How you mow during summer has a profound impact on your lawn's ability to withstand heat, drought, and pest pressure. Mowing might seem like a simple chore, but improper techniques during hot weather can cause lasting damage that takes weeks or months to repair.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Raise Your Mowing Height</h3>\\n\\n<p>One of the most impactful changes you can make during hot summer months is raising your mower blade. Taller grass provides several critical benefits during heat waves:</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Shades the soil:</strong> Longer grass blades cast shadows over the soil surface, reducing soil temperature by as much as 10 to 15 degrees and slowing moisture evaporation.</li>\\n<li><strong>Promotes deeper roots:</strong> There is a direct correlation between blade height and root depth. Taller grass supports deeper, more resilient root systems.</li>\\n<li><strong>Suppresses weeds:</strong> Dense, tall grass shades out weed seedlings, preventing crabgrass and other summer weeds from establishing.</li>\\n<li><strong>Increases photosynthesis:</strong> More leaf surface area means more energy production, helping the grass recover from stress faster.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p>As a general rule, raise your mowing height by half an inch to a full inch above your normal setting during summer. For cool-season grasses like <a href=\\\"/grass-types/tall-fescue\\\">tall fescue</a>, aim for 3.5 to 4 inches. Warm-season grasses like <a href=\\\"/grass-types/bermuda-grass\\\">Bermuda grass</a> can be maintained at 2 to 2.5 inches, while <a href=\\\"/grass-types/zoysia-grass\\\">Zoysia</a> performs well at 2 to 3 inches during peak heat.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Follow the One-Third Rule</h3>\\n\\n<p>Never remove more than one-third of the grass blade in a single mowing session. Cutting too much at once shocks the plant, reduces its ability to photosynthesize, and exposes tender stem tissue to scorching sunlight. If your grass has grown too tall between mowings, reduce the height gradually over several sessions spaced a few days apart.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Keep Your Mower Blades Sharp</h3>\\n\\n<p>Dull mower blades tear grass instead of cutting it cleanly, leaving ragged tips that turn brown and become more susceptible to disease. During summer stress, this damage is amplified. Sharpen your mower blades at least once per month during the growing season, or whenever you notice frayed grass tips after mowing.</p>\\n\\n<p>Consider investing in a mulching mower or mulching blade. Leaving grass clippings on the lawn returns valuable nitrogen and organic matter to the soil, acts as a light mulch to retain moisture, and saves you time and effort. Contrary to popular belief, clippings do not contribute to thatch buildup when mowed regularly. For a deeper dive into proper mowing techniques for every season, visit our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/lawn-mowing-guide\\\">lawn mowing guide</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Adjust Mowing Frequency</h3>\\n\\n<p>During periods of extreme heat or drought, your grass growth slows considerably. Resist the urge to mow on your usual schedule if the grass hasn't grown enough to warrant it. Mowing stressed grass causes unnecessary damage. Let your lawn dictate the mowing schedule rather than the calendar.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Fertilizing &amp; Soil Care During Hot Weather</h2>\\n\\n<p>Fertilizing during hot summer months requires careful judgment. While your lawn does need nutrients to maintain health and recover from stress, applying the wrong type or amount of fertilizer at the wrong time can do more harm than good.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Know Your Grass Type's Summer Needs</h3>\\n\\n<p>Warm-season grasses such as <a href=\\\"/grass-types/bermuda-grass\\\">Bermuda</a>, <a href=\\\"/grass-types/zoysia-grass\\\">Zoysia</a>, <a href=\\\"/grass-types/st-augustine-grass\\\">St. Augustine</a>, and <a href=\\\"/grass-types/centipede-grass\\\">centipede</a> are actively growing during summer and can benefit from light, strategic fertilization. Cool-season grasses like fescue, bluegrass, and ryegrass enter a semi-dormant state during extreme heat and should generally <em>not</em> be fertilized during the hottest weeks.</p>\\n\\n<p>Applying high-nitrogen fertilizer to cool-season grasses during summer forces tender new growth that is extremely vulnerable to heat damage and disease. It can also burn the lawn if applied during drought conditions. Save your major fertilization for early spring and fall when cool-season grasses are actively growing.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Use Slow-Release or Organic Fertilizers</h3>\\n\\n<p>If you do fertilize warm-season grasses during summer, choose slow-release or organic fertilizers over quick-release synthetic options. Slow-release formulas feed your lawn gradually over several weeks, reducing the risk of burning and providing a steady supply of nutrients. Look for products containing:</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Methylene urea or sulfur-coated urea</strong> for controlled nitrogen release</li>\\n<li><strong>Milorganite or other biosolid-based products</strong> for gentle, organic feeding</li>\\n<li><strong>Seaweed or kelp extracts</strong> for natural stress relief and micronutrients</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p>Always water thoroughly after applying any granular fertilizer to move it into the soil and prevent leaf burn.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Soil Health: The Foundation of a Heat-Resistant Lawn</h3>\\n\\n<p>Healthy soil produces healthy grass that can better withstand summer stress. Consider these soil care practices:</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Core aeration:</strong> If you missed your spring aeration window, early summer aeration is acceptable for warm-season grasses. This relieves compaction and improves water infiltration. Learn more in our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/lawn-aeration-guide\\\">lawn aeration guide</a>.</li>\\n<li><strong>Top-dressing with compost:</strong> A thin layer (one-quarter inch) of quality compost improves soil structure, adds beneficial microbes, and enhances moisture retention.</li>\\n<li><strong>Soil testing:</strong> A soil test reveals pH imbalances and nutrient deficiencies that may be limiting your lawn's heat tolerance. Most grasses thrive at a pH between 6.0 and 7.0.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>Humic Acid &amp; Soil Conditioners</h3>\\n\\n<p>Humic acid applications during summer can improve nutrient uptake, enhance root development, and help grass cope with heat stress. Liquid humic acid products are easy to apply with a hose-end sprayer and are compatible with most fertilizers. Many lawn care professionals consider humic acid a secret weapon for maintaining green lawns through brutal summers.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Pest, Weed &amp; Disease Management in Summer</h2>\\n\\n<p>Hot, humid summer weather creates ideal conditions for a variety of lawn pests, weeds, and diseases. Staying ahead of these problems is far easier than trying to fix damage after it occurs. A proactive approach to summer lawn threats will keep your yard looking its best.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Common Summer Lawn Pests</h3>\\n\\n<p>Several insects become particularly active during hot summer months:</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Grubs:</strong> The larvae of Japanese beetles and other beetles feed on grass roots during mid to late summer, causing irregular brown patches that can be rolled back like carpet. Apply a preventive grub control product containing chlorantraniliprole or imidacloprid in early summer for season-long protection.</li>\\n<li><strong>Chinch bugs:</strong> These tiny pests are especially destructive to <a href=\\\"/grass-types/st-augustine-grass\\\">St. Augustine grass</a> and <a href=\\\"/grass-types/bermuda-grass\\\">Bermuda grass</a> during hot, dry weather. Look for yellowing patches near driveways and sidewalks where heat radiates. Targeted insecticide applications or beneficial nematodes can control infestations.</li>\\n<li><strong>Armyworms:</strong> These caterpillars can devastate a lawn in a matter of days, chewing grass blades down to the stem. They're most active during late summer. Bt (Bacillus thuringiensis) products offer an effective organic control option.</li>\\n<li><strong>Sod webworms:</strong> Active at night, these larvae chew grass blades and leave behind small brown patches and silk-like webbing on the soil surface.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>Summer Weed Control</h3>\\n\\n<p>Summer annual weeds like crabgrass, goosegrass, and spurge thrive in hot weather, especially in thin or bare areas of the lawn. The best defense against summer weeds is a thick, healthy lawn maintained with proper mowing and watering practices.</p>\\n\\n<p>If weeds do appear, spot-treat them with a selective post-emergent herbicide rather than applying broad-spectrum products across the entire lawn. Be cautious when applying herbicides during extreme heat (above 85°F / 29°C), as many products can damage desirable grass when temperatures are high. Apply herbicides in the early morning or wait for a cooler day. For comprehensive strategies, read our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/weed-control-guide\\\">weed control guide</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Fungal Diseases to Watch For</h3>\\n\\n<p>Summer fungal diseases thrive in hot, humid conditions and can spread rapidly:</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Brown patch:</strong> Circular brown areas ranging from a few inches to several feet across, often with a dark ring at the edge. Most common in tall fescue and ryegrass during humid weather.</li>\\n<li><strong>Dollar spot:</strong> Small, silver-dollar-sized bleached spots that can merge into larger patches. Affects nearly all grass types and is worsened by low nitrogen levels and leaf wetness.</li>\\n<li><strong>Pythium blight:</strong> A fast-moving disease that creates greasy, dark patches, often following drainage patterns. Most destructive during hot, wet weather.</li>\\n<li><strong>Fairy ring:</strong> Arcs or rings of dark green or dead grass, sometimes accompanied by mushrooms. Difficult to control but primarily cosmetic.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p>Prevention is always the best strategy. Maintain proper watering habits (morning only, deep and infrequent), avoid excessive nitrogen during humid periods, and improve air circulation by trimming surrounding vegetation. If disease does strike, fungicide applications containing azoxystrobin or propiconazole can halt the spread. Our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/lawn-disease-identification\\\">lawn disease identification guide</a> can help you diagnose specific problems.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Essential Tools &amp; Products for Summer Lawn Care</h2>\\n\\n<p>Having the right tools and products on hand makes summer lawn care more effective and efficient. Here's a curated list of items every homeowner should consider for maintaining a healthy lawn during hot weather.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Irrigation Tools</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Oscillating or rotary sprinklers:</strong> Choose sprinklers that provide even coverage and match them to your lawn's shape and size. Impact sprinklers are excellent for large, open areas.</li>\\n<li><strong>Smart irrigation controller:</strong> WiFi-enabled controllers adjust watering schedules based on local weather data, soil moisture, and evapotranspiration rates, saving water and preventing overwatering.</li>\\n<li><strong>Soaker hoses:</strong> Ideal for targeted watering of stressed areas or newly seeded patches.</li>\\n<li><strong>Rain gauge:</strong> An inexpensive but invaluable tool for tracking how much water your lawn receives from both rainfall and irrigation.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>Mowing Equipment</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Quality mower with adjustable deck:</strong> Whether you prefer push, self-propelled, or riding mowers, ensure the cutting height is easily adjustable so you can raise the blade for summer.</li>\\n<li><strong>Spare mower blades:</strong> Keep a sharp backup blade on hand so you can swap them out quickly and send the dull blade for sharpening.</li>\\n<li><strong>Grass catcher or mulching kit:</strong> Depending on your preference, having both options gives you flexibility during varying growth rates.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>Soil &amp; Fertilizer Products</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Slow-release granular fertilizer:</strong> Look for formulations designed for summer use with lower nitrogen ratios, such as 10-0-10 or similar balanced blends.</li>\\n<li><strong>Liquid humic acid:</strong> An excellent soil amendment that enhances root health and stress tolerance.</li>\\n<li><strong>Wetting agents (surfactants):</strong> These products break water's surface tension, helping it penetrate dry, hydrophobic soil — a common problem in summer. They're especially useful for sloped areas or soils high in clay content.</li>\\n<li><strong>Organic compost:</strong> For top-dressing and improving soil biology.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>Monitoring &amp; Diagnostic Tools</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Soil moisture meter:</strong> A simple probe that tells you exactly how wet the soil is at root depth, eliminating watering guesswork.</li>\\n<li><strong>Soil test kit:</strong> Test your soil at least once per year to understand pH and nutrient levels. Many university extension offices offer affordable soil testing services.</li>\\n<li><strong>Hand lens or magnifying glass:</strong> Helpful for identifying small pests like chinch bugs and mites before infestations become severe.</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h2>Seasonal Timing Guide for Summer Lawn Care</h2>\\n\\n<p>Timing matters enormously in lawn care. Here's a month-by-month breakdown of key tasks to keep your lawn healthy throughout the summer season.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Early Summer (Late Spring Transition)</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li>Raise mower blade to summer height recommendations for your grass type</li>\\n<li>Apply preventive grub control if not done in spring</li>\\n<li>Transition to deep, infrequent watering schedule</li>\\n<li>Apply pre-emergent herbicide if crabgrass has been a recurring problem (timing depends on soil temperature reaching 55°F / 13°C)</li>\\n<li>Sharpen mower blades and service equipment</li>\\n<li>Apply a light round of slow-release fertilizer for warm-season grasses</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>Mid-Summer (Peak Heat)</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li>Monitor soil moisture closely and adjust watering as needed</li>\\n<li>Reduce or eliminate fertilization, especially for cool-season grasses</li>\\n<li>Scout weekly for signs of insect damage and disease</li>\\n<li>Avoid heavy foot traffic on stressed areas</li>\\n<li>Do not aerate or dethatch cool-season lawns during this period</li>\\n<li>Spot-treat weeds carefully, avoiding applications during extreme heat</li>\\n<li>Apply humic acid or seaweed extract for stress relief</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>Late Summer (Preparing for Fall Recovery)</h3>\\n\\n<ul>\\n<li>Begin planning fall overseeding and aeration for cool-season lawns</li>\\n<li>Apply fall pre-emergent herbicide as soil temperatures begin to drop</li>\\n<li>Continue monitoring for late-season pests like armyworms and sod webworms</li>\\n<li>Gradually lower mowing height back toward normal levels as temperatures moderate</li>\\n<li>Start preparing your fall fertilization plan — the most important feeding of the year for cool-season grasses</li>\\n<li>Order grass seed and supplies for fall <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/overseeding-your-lawn\\\">overseeding</a></li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p>Understanding this seasonal rhythm helps you stay ahead of problems and gives your lawn the support it needs at each stage of the summer. For a complete year-round calendar, visit our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/seasonal-lawn-care-calendar\\\">seasonal lawn care calendar</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Summer Lawn Care</h2>\\n\\n<h3>Should I water my lawn every day during a heat wave?</h3>\\n\\n<p>No, daily watering is generally not recommended even during heat waves. Watering deeply two to three times per week is more effective because it encourages deep root growth. However, if you notice severe wilting, footprinting, or a bluish-gray color, a light \\\"syringing\\\" (brief watering to cool the grass canopy) during the hottest part of the afternoon can provide temporary relief without promoting shallow roots. This should be a short, 5 to 10 minute session — not a full watering cycle. The key is to cool the grass blades without saturating the soil.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Why is my grass turning brown in summer even though I'm watering it?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Brown grass during summer can result from several causes beyond insufficient water. Cool-season grasses naturally enter summer dormancy when temperatures consistently exceed 85°F (29°C), and this dormancy causes browning that is a survival mechanism, not a sign of death. The grass will green up again when temperatures cool. Other potential causes include fungal diseases like brown patch, grub damage (test by tugging on the grass — if it lifts easily, grubs have eaten the roots), dull mower blades tearing the grass, or compacted soil preventing water from reaching the root zone. A soil moisture probe can help you determine if the soil is actually dry or if another issue is at play.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Is it okay to fertilize my lawn during a drought?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Generally, you should avoid fertilizing during drought conditions. Fertilizer requires water to dissolve and move into the root zone, and applying it to dry soil can burn grass roots and blades. Additionally, fertilizing stimulates new growth that demands more water than the grass can access during a drought, putting additional stress on the plant. Wait until rainfall resumes or your regular irrigation schedule has restored adequate soil moisture before applying fertilizer. The one exception is liquid humic acid or seaweed extract, which can be applied at low rates during drought to help plants cope with stress without promoting excessive growth.</p>\\n\\n<h3>How short should I cut my grass in the summer?</h3>\\n\\n<p>You should cut your grass <em>taller</em> in summer, not shorter. For cool-season grasses, maintain a height of 3 to 4 inches during hot weather. For warm-season grasses, 2 to 3 inches is appropriate depending on the species. Taller grass shades the soil, reduces evaporation, suppresses weeds, and supports deeper root systems. Never remove more than one-third of the blade in a single mowing, and always keep your mower blades sharp to ensure clean cuts that heal quickly. If you're unsure about the ideal height for your specific grass type, consult our <a href=\\\"/grass-types\\\">grass types guide</a> for detailed information on each variety.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Can I lay new sod or plant grass seed during the summer?</h3>\\n\\n<p>While it's possible to install sod during summer, it's not ideal and requires intensive management. New sod must be watered frequently — often two to four times daily for the first two weeks — to prevent the roots from drying out before they establish. This uses a tremendous amount of water and demands vigilant attention. Grass seed is even more challenging to establish in summer heat, as seedlings are extremely vulnerable to drying out and heat stress. The optimal time for seeding cool-season grasses is early fall, while warm-season grasses are best seeded in late spring to early summer. If you must install sod during peak heat, choose heat-tolerant varieties like <a href=\\\"/grass-types/bermuda-grass\\\">Bermuda</a> or <a href=\\\"/grass-types/zoysia-grass\\\">Zoysia</a>, and be prepared to commit to an aggressive watering schedule for at least three to four weeks. For detailed establishment tips, see our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/how-to-lay-sod\\\">guide to laying sod</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Final Thoughts on Summer Lawn Care</h2>\\n\\n<p>Maintaining a healthy lawn during hot summer weather comes down to understanding what your grass needs and adjusting your practices accordingly. Deep, infrequent watering in the early morning. Taller mowing heights that shade the soil and protect the crown. Smart fertilization that feeds without burning. Vigilant pest and disease monitoring. These fundamentals, applied consistently, will carry your lawn through even the most punishing summer heat.</p>\\n\\n<p>Remember that some browning during extreme heat is natural, especially for cool-season grasses. Dormancy is a survival strategy, not a death sentence. As long as the crown of the plant remains firm and white, your grass is alive and will recover when conditions improve. Focus on keeping the plant alive rather than chasing perfect green color during the hottest weeks.</p>\\n\\n<p>By following the strategies outlined in this guide, you'll build a lawn that is not just surviving summer but thriving through it — with deep roots, resilient growth, and the vigor to bounce back quickly when cooler weather returns. For more lawn care advice tailored to every season and grass type, explore our complete library of <a href=\\\"/lawn-care\\\">lawn care guides</a> and start building the yard of your dreams.</p>\",\"tag\":null,\"is_online\":\"Y\",\"subtype\":null,\"slug\":null,\"cover_image\":null,\"status\":\"published\",\"created_at\":\"2026-08-28 00:35:21\"}","status":200,"url":""},"revalidate":3600,"tags":[]}